high enough up in altitude where the flaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:53.520
still aren't effective, but you're low&nbsp;
enough where you have arrow forces happening

00:01:53.520 --> 00:02:00.000
and then suddenly you have this hypersonic airflow&nbsp;
hitting your jet and it rips the jet apart.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.280
and you lose effectiveness. And NASA has&nbsp;
even seen that for certain tests. Um,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:08.400
you, you know, you imagine from&nbsp;
a Newton's laws perspective,

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.800
you fire a jet one way, you move&nbsp;
the other way kind of thing.&nbsp;

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:15.120
That interaction gets so complex that sometimes&nbsp;
you fire the jet, you move the same direction.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:21.480
That’s a major problem for the new wave of&nbsp;
hypersonic aircraft being developed. You may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:27.360
surprised to learn that ICBMS travel MUCH faster&nbsp;
than any hypersonic vehicles being developed.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:33.300
Intercontinental ballistic missiles are designed&nbsp;
to exit earth's atmosphere, travel extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:40.560
quickly at speeds of up to 7.8 km/s through&nbsp;
space and then suddenly dive on their targets.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.640
They move quickly, but their lofted&nbsp;
trajectory gives their enemies more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.820
time to detect and aim anti-missile defenses.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:52.020
